00:00:42.874 --> 00:00:44.794
Introduction and Why It Matters.

12
00:00:46.044 --> 00:00:50.097
A story: the PostgreSQL table that ate the orders database.

13
00:00:50.377 --> 00:00:59.679
When the delivery fleet reached 30,000 riders, the team stored location pings the familiar way, as rows in PostgreSQL next to the orders:

14
00:01:01.029 --> 00:01:02.629
Here is the SQL code.

15
00:01:05.479 --> 00:01:14.417
At 7,500 inserts per second, with two indexes and 90 days of retention, the table reached 2 billion rows in three months.

16
00:01:14.697 --> 00:01:16.319
Autovacuum couldn't keep up.

17
00:01:16.599 --> 00:01:24.001
The WAL volume from location inserts saturated replication, so order-history replicas lagged by minutes.

18
00:01:24.281 --> 00:01:25.497
A nightly DELETE...

19
00:01:25.777 --> 00:01:30.428
WHERE recordedat now() - interval '90 days' locked up I or O for hours.

20
00:01:30.708 --> 00:01:39.327
During one festival evening, checkout latency tripled because orders and locations shared a disk, a WAL stream and a buffer cache.

21
00:01:40.677 --> 00:01:42.767
The data wasn't relational at all.

22
00:01:43.047 --> 00:01:45.330
Nobody joined locations with anything.

23
00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:52.159
Every read was either where is rider X now? which riders are near store Y? or rider X's path today.

24
00:01:52.439 --> 00:01:59.458
Those are three access patterns, and each fits a different storage model better than a B-tree table on the orders primary:

25
00:02:00.808 --> 00:02:02.152
