00:36:06.054 --> 00:36:06.801
Warning:

766
00:36:07.081 --> 00:36:08.809
Hash tags concentrate load.

767
00:36:09.089 --> 00:36:12.353
A city-level hash tag puts a whole city on one shard.

768
00:36:12.633 --> 00:36:23.790
At ~20 K Bengaluru riders times 1 ping or 4 s about 5 K pings per second (batched into ~50 Lua calls per second), one shard is comfortable.

769
00:36:24.070 --> 00:36:31.409
For a mega-city beyond one shard's capacity, split the tag by zone (BLR-south) and search the neighbouring zones.

770
00:36:32.759 --> 00:36:33.633
Decision:

771
00:36:33.913 --> 00:36:36.367
Redis for latest and geo (clear winner).

772
00:36:36.647 --> 00:36:43.623
For history, a managed Cassandra-compatible service (35) narrowly beats DynamoDB (34).

773
00:36:43.903 --> 00:36:49.151
Self-run Cassandra would score 33 and lose, which shows how much operability matters.

774
00:36:49.431 --> 00:36:54.017
The deciding factor was sustained write cost at 40 K writes per second.

775
00:36:54.297 --> 00:37:00.356
DynamoDB wins if the platform standardizes on AWS or write volume drops.

776
00:37:00.636 --> 00:37:08.167
A near-tie in the matrix is a signal to name the deciding factor explicitly in the ADR, not to trust the arithmetic.

