967
00:51:10.821 --> 00:51:11.738
Context.

968
00:51:12.018 --> 00:51:17.693
Location pings in PostgreSQL degraded the orders database (§1.1).

969
00:51:17.973 --> 00:51:23.839
L3's synchronous dual write couples ingest latency to the slowest store and can't replay.

970
00:51:24.119 --> 00:51:24.994
Decision.

971
00:51:25.274 --> 00:51:36.730
(1) Ingest publishes each ping once to tracking.location.updated.v1 (key = riderId, acks=all, idempotent producer, 3-day retention).

972
00:51:37.010 --> 00:51:50.130
(2) Independent consumer groups feed Redis (latest + geo, Lua batch with seq guard), Cassandra (7-day history, day-bucketed, TTL + TWCS, LOCALQUORUM), and analytics.

973
00:51:50.410 --> 00:51:53.845
(3) Sinks commit offsets only after successful writes.

974
00:51:54.125 --> 00:51:57.495
Writes are idempotent, so at-least-once delivery is safe.

975
00:51:57.775 --> 00:52:05.306
(4) Malformed messages go to a DLT, and transient failures retry with backoff (lag grows, data is safe).

976
00:52:05.586 --> 00:52:08.637
(5) Rebuild by resetting consumer-group offsets.

977
00:52:09.987 --> 00:52:11.011
Consequences.

978
00:52:11.291 --> 00:52:15.280
(+) A slow or broken store only delays its own consumer.

979
00:52:15.560 --> 00:52:18.952
(+) New consumers can be added without touching ingest.

980
00:52:19.232 --> 00:52:21.963
(+) Stores are rebuildable within retention.

981
00:52:22.243 --> 00:52:28.344
(minus ) Kafka is now critical for tracking (clients buffer locally when ingest is unavailable).

982
00:52:28.624 --> 00:52:33.083
(minus ) Eventual consistency between Redis and Cassandra (seconds).

983
00:52:33.363 --> 00:52:38.547
(minus ) A new technology (Cassandra) to operate, mitigated by a managed service.

984
00:52:38.827 --> 00:52:51.861
Revisit when: history retention requirements exceed ~30 days (consider tiered storage or object store), or geo queries need polygons and zones (H3 indexes, Ch 47).

