1017
00:53:51.967 --> 00:53:52.841
L4:

1018
00:53:53.121 --> 00:53:55.553
Single write to the log; independent sinks.

1019
00:53:55.833 --> 00:53:56.751
Recovery.

1020
00:53:57.031 --> 00:53:57.884
L3:

1021
00:53:58.164 --> 00:53:58.975
Manual.

1022
00:53:59.255 --> 00:54:00.129
L4:

1023
00:54:00.409 --> 00:54:03.353
Offset reset, rebuild, DLT for poison.

1024
00:54:04.703 --> 00:54:05.642
Part 6.

1025
00:54:05.922 --> 00:54:07.202
Production Concerns.

1026
00:54:08.452 --> 00:54:09.604
Failure modes.

1027
00:54:09.884 --> 00:54:11.228
Here is a quick table:

1028
00:54:11.508 --> 00:54:15.391
Failure, and Detection signal, and Blast radius, and Mitigation.

1029
00:54:15.671 --> 00:54:18.700
Hot partition (Cassandra) / hot key (Redis).

1030
00:54:18.980 --> 00:54:20.175
Detection signal:

1031
00:54:20.455 --> 00:54:26.535
One node's CPU or latency far above others; nodetool toppartitions; Redis --hotkeys.

1032
00:54:26.815 --> 00:54:28.031
Blast radius:

1033
00:54:28.311 --> 00:54:31.105
Latency for everything on that node or shard.

1034
00:54:31.385 --> 00:54:32.388
Mitigation:

1035
00:54:32.668 --> 00:54:36.337
Better partition keys (bucketing), key splitting, local caching.

1036
00:54:36.617 --> 00:54:38.260
Unbounded partition growth.

1037
00:54:38.540 --> 00:54:44.300
Detection signal: nodetool tablehistograms partition size p99 rising.

1038
00:54:44.580 --> 00:54:45.796
Blast radius:

1039
00:54:46.076 --> 00:54:48.828
Slow reads, compaction pain, repair failures.

1040
00:54:49.108 --> 00:54:50.111
Mitigation:

1041
00:54:50.391 --> 00:54:54.401
Time buckets in partition keys; enforce limits in code reviews.

1042
00:54:54.681 --> 00:54:56.281
Tombstone accumulation.

1043
00:54:56.561 --> 00:54:57.756
Detection signal:

1044
00:54:58.036 --> 00:55:02.132
TombstoneOverwhelmingException, slow reads, warnings in logs.

1045
00:55:02.412 --> 00:55:03.628
Blast radius:

1046
00:55:03.908 --> 00:55:04.975
Queries fail.

1047
00:55:05.255 --> 00:55:06.257
Mitigation:

1048
00:55:06.537 --> 00:55:13.919
Avoid delete-heavy or queue patterns; TTL + TWCS; correct gcgraceseconds with repairs.

1049
00:55:15.269 --> 00:55:16.591
Compaction backlog.

1050
00:55:16.871 --> 00:55:18.066
Detection signal:

1051
00:55:18.346 --> 00:55:22.378
Pending compactions ↑, read latency ↑, disk ↑.

1052
00:55:22.658 --> 00:55:23.874
Blast radius:

1053
00:55:24.154 --> 00:55:25.434
Node degradation.

1054
00:55:25.714 --> 00:55:26.717
Mitigation:

1055
00:55:26.997 --> 00:55:32.586
Right strategy per table; throughput limits; capacity headroom (disk 50 to 60 %).

1056
00:55:32.866 --> 00:55:35.127
Replica divergence (missed repairs).

1057
00:55:35.407 --> 00:55:36.602
Detection signal:

1058
00:55:36.882 --> 00:55:39.549
Repair failures; inconsistent reads at ONE.

1059
00:55:39.829 --> 00:55:41.045
Blast radius:

1060
00:55:41.325 --> 00:55:47.170
Stale or resurrected data (deleted data returning if repairs lapse beyond gcgrace).

1061
00:55:47.450 --> 00:55:48.453
Mitigation:

1062
00:55:48.733 --> 00:55:51.421
Scheduled incremental repair; monitoring.

1063
00:55:51.701 --> 00:55:53.322
Redis failover data loss.

1064
00:55:53.602 --> 00:55:54.797
Detection signal:

1065
00:55:55.077 --> 00:55:58.021
Replication offset gap; missing recent keys.

1066
00:55:58.301 --> 00:55:59.517
Blast radius:

1067
00:55:59.797 --> 00:56:01.546
Last ~seconds of writes.

1068
00:56:01.826 --> 00:56:02.829
Mitigation:

1069
00:56:03.109 --> 00:56:09.445
Use Redis only for rebuildable data; WAIT for critical writes (reduces, doesn't remove, the risk).

1070
00:56:10.795 --> 00:56:12.395
Redis memory exhaustion.

1071
00:56:12.675 --> 00:56:16.344
Detection signal: usedmemory near maxmemory; evictions.

1072
00:56:16.624 --> 00:56:17.840
Blast radius:

1073
00:56:18.120 --> 00:56:20.915
Evicted keys (or write errors with noeviction).

1074
00:56:21.195 --> 00:56:22.197
Mitigation:

1075
00:56:22.477 --> 00:56:30.072
Eviction policy chosen per use (volatile-ttl for caches, noeviction for state), TTLs everywhere, sizing.

1076
00:56:30.352 --> 00:56:31.589
Long Lua scripts.

1077
00:56:31.869 --> 00:56:33.064
Detection signal:

1078
00:56:33.344 --> 00:56:35.499
Redis latency spikes (SLOWLOG).

1079
00:56:35.779 --> 00:56:36.995
Blast radius:

1080
00:56:37.275 --> 00:56:38.555
Whole shard blocked.

1081
00:56:38.835 --> 00:56:39.837
Mitigation:

1082
00:56:40.117 --> 00:56:42.464
Bounded batches; no unbounded loops.

1083
00:56:42.744 --> 00:56:44.728
CROSSSLOT errors in Redis Cluster.

1084
00:56:45.008 --> 00:56:46.203
Detection signal:

1085
00:56:46.483 --> 00:56:49.597
Errors on multi-key ops after moving to cluster.

1086
00:56:49.877 --> 00:56:51.093
Blast radius:

1087
00:56:51.373 --> 00:56:53.144
Features break at migration.

1088
00:56:53.424 --> 00:56:54.427
Mitigation:

1089
00:56:54.707 --> 00:56:56.456
Hash tags designed up front.

1090
00:56:56.736 --> 00:56:58.613
Sink lag (Kafka consumers).

1091
00:56:58.893 --> 00:57:00.088
Detection signal:

1092
00:57:00.368 --> 00:57:01.797
Consumer lag per group.

1093
00:57:02.077 --> 00:57:03.293
Blast radius:

1094
00:57:03.573 --> 00:57:05.109
That sink's freshness only.

1095
00:57:05.389 --> 00:57:06.392
Mitigation:

1096
00:57:06.672 --> 00:57:11.472
Independent groups; autoscale consumers; alert on lag versus retention.

1097
00:57:12.822 --> 00:57:14.059
Poison messages.

1098
00:57:14.339 --> 00:57:15.534
Detection signal:

1099
00:57:15.814 --> 00:57:17.947
DLT growth; stuck partition.

1100
00:57:18.227 --> 00:57:19.443
Blast radius:

1101
00:57:19.723 --> 00:57:21.089
One partition stalls.

1102
00:57:21.369 --> 00:57:22.371
Mitigation:

1103
00:57:22.651 --> 00:57:27.814
Non-retryable classification, then DLT; schema validation at ingest.

1104
00:57:28.094 --> 00:57:29.971
Locale-dependent serialization.

1105
00:57:30.251 --> 00:57:31.446
Detection signal:

1106
00:57:31.726 --> 00:57:35.011
DLT spike after a deploy to a new base image.

1107
00:57:35.291 --> 00:57:36.507
Blast radius:

1108
00:57:36.787 --> 00:57:37.854
Every message.

1109
00:57:38.134 --> 00:57:39.137
Mitigation:

1110
00:57:39.417 --> 00:57:44.302
Locale.ROOT for machine formats; proper JSON or Avro serializers.

1111
00:57:45.652 --> 00:57:46.740
Observability.

1112
00:57:47.020 --> 00:57:48.364
Here is a quick table:

1113
00:57:48.644 --> 00:57:50.095
Store, and Key metrics.

1114
00:57:50.375 --> 00:58:03.089
Redis: ops per second, latency (p99 via LATENCY or client metrics), memory, evictions, keyspace hits or misses, replication offset or lag, SLOWLOG, connected clients.

1115
00:58:03.369 --> 00:58:19.796
Cassandra or ScyllaDB: read or write latency p99 per table, timeouts, unavailable errors, pending compactions, SSTables per read, tombstones per read, partition size histograms, disk usage, dropped messages, repair status.

1116
00:58:20.076 --> 00:58:29.505
Document DB (MongoDB): op latencies, query targeting (scanned or returned), replication lag, WiredTiger cache, slow query log.

1117
00:58:29.785 --> 00:58:36.505
Pipeline: consumer lag per group, DLT rate, end-to-end freshness (event ts, then visible in store).

1118
00:58:36.785 --> 00:58:42.759
Business: stale-rider count, pings.ignoredoutoforder, nearby query result sizes per store.

1119
00:58:44.109 --> 00:58:45.047
Security.

1120
00:58:45.327 --> 00:58:48.805
Network isolation: data stores in private subnets.

1121
00:58:49.085 --> 00:58:51.175
Redis should never be internet-exposed.

1122
00:58:51.455 --> 00:59:01.311
Use AUTH or ACLs even internally (Redis 6 plus ACL users per service with command restrictions, and no FLUSHALL or KEYS in app users).

1123
00:59:01.591 --> 00:59:05.879
TLS in transit for Redis, Cassandra and MongoDB clients.

1124
00:59:06.159 --> 00:59:08.271
Authentication and least privilege:

1125
00:59:08.551 --> 00:59:11.794
Cassandra roles per service with keyspace-level grants.

1126
00:59:12.074 --> 00:59:14.122
MongoDB users per database.

1127
00:59:15.472 --> 00:59:24.389
Injection: document stores have injection risks too (MongoDB operator injection via $where/$ne in user input).

1128
00:59:24.669 --> 00:59:28.552
Build queries with typed APIs, never string concatenation.

1129
00:59:28.832 --> 00:59:34.187
The same goes for Lua scripts: pass data as ARGV, never interpolate it into script text.

1130
00:59:34.467 --> 00:59:39.267
PII and location privacy: rider location is personal data.

1131
00:59:39.547 --> 00:59:50.917
Set retention (TTL 7 days), restrict access (support tools audited), and exclude it from analytics exports unless aggregated (DPDP, Ch 36).

1132
00:59:51.197 --> 00:59:54.568
Backups and encryption at rest according to data class.

1133
00:59:54.848 --> 01:00:00.096
Derived stores can rely on rebuild, but the log (Kafka) and SoR need backups.

1134
01:00:01.446 --> 01:00:03.110
Performance and tuning knobs.

1135
01:00:03.390 --> 01:00:04.734
Here is a quick table:

1136
01:00:05.014 --> 01:00:06.849
Knob, and Value, and Guidance.

1137
01:00:07.129 --> 01:00:08.643
Cassandra consistency.

1138
01:00:08.923 --> 01:00:09.691
Value:

1139
01:00:09.971 --> 01:00:11.358
LOCALQUORUM default.

1140
01:00:11.638 --> 01:00:12.534
Guidance:

1141
01:00:12.814 --> 01:00:18.019
LOCALONE for tolerant writes after measuring; never cross-DC QUORUM on hot paths.

1142
01:00:18.299 --> 01:00:19.686
Compaction strategy.

1143
01:00:19.966 --> 01:00:20.734
Value:

1144
01:00:21.014 --> 01:00:26.710
TWCS for TTL time series; LCS read-heavy; STCS general.

1145
01:00:26.990 --> 01:00:27.886
Guidance:

1146
01:00:28.166 --> 01:00:31.771
Wrong strategy is the most common Cassandra performance issue.

1147
01:00:32.051 --> 01:00:33.609
Partition size target.

1148
01:00:33.889 --> 01:00:34.657
Value:

1149
01:00:34.937 --> 01:00:38.051
10 MB typical, well below 100 MB.

1150
01:00:38.331 --> 01:00:39.227
Guidance:

1151
01:00:39.507 --> 01:00:41.449
Bucket by time or hash suffix.

1152
01:00:41.729 --> 01:00:42.518
Driver.

1153
01:00:42.798 --> 01:00:43.566
Value:

1154
01:00:43.846 --> 01:00:48.710
Prepared statements, token-aware routing, setIdempotent(true), bounded in-flight.

1155
01:00:48.990 --> 01:00:49.886
Guidance:

1156
01:00:50.166 --> 01:00:53.622
Speculative execution only for idempotent queries.

1157
01:00:54.972 --> 01:00:57.020
Redis pipelining or Lua batching.

1158
01:00:57.300 --> 01:00:58.068
Value:

1159
01:00:58.348 --> 01:01:00.673
50 to 200 ops per round trip.

1160
01:01:00.953 --> 01:01:01.849
Guidance:

1161
01:01:02.129 --> 01:01:04.199
Latency and CPU savings.

1162
01:01:04.479 --> 01:01:06.143
Redis maxmemory-policy.

1163
01:01:06.423 --> 01:01:07.191
Value:

1164
01:01:07.471 --> 01:01:11.545
Caches: allkeys-lru or volatile-lfu; state: noeviction.

1165
01:01:11.825 --> 01:01:12.721
Guidance:

1166
01:01:13.001 --> 01:01:14.985
Decide per instance, not per key.

1167
01:01:15.265 --> 01:01:16.481
Redis persistence.

1168
01:01:16.761 --> 01:01:17.529
Value:

1169
01:01:17.809 --> 01:01:19.900
Caches: none or RDB; state:

1170
01:01:20.180 --> 01:01:21.439
AOF everysec.

1171
01:01:21.719 --> 01:01:22.615
Guidance:

1172
01:01:22.895 --> 01:01:24.687
Durability versus latency.

1173
01:01:24.967 --> 01:01:27.313
Kafka consumer max.poll.records.

1174
01:01:27.593 --> 01:01:28.361
Value:

1175
01:01:28.641 --> 01:01:29.623
500.

1176
01:01:29.903 --> 01:01:30.799
Guidance:

1177
01:01:31.079 --> 01:01:35.580
Batch size versus processing time within max.poll.interval.ms.

1178
01:01:35.860 --> 01:01:37.119
GEO search COUNT.

1179
01:01:37.399 --> 01:01:38.167
Value:

1180
01:01:38.447 --> 01:01:39.535
Small (20).

1181
01:01:39.815 --> 01:01:40.711
Guidance:

1182
01:01:40.991 --> 01:01:42.463
Bounded work per query.

1183
01:01:43.813 --> 01:01:44.538
Cost.

1184
01:01:44.818 --> 01:01:46.162
Here is a quick table:

1185
01:01:46.442 --> 01:01:47.679
Driver, and Note.

1186
01:01:47.959 --> 01:01:55.405
Redis memory: ~20 MB for latest locations is trivial; carts and sessions dominate Redis costs.

1187
01:01:55.685 --> 01:01:58.650
Managed Redis is priced per GB-hour of memory.

1188
01:01:58.930 --> 01:02:01.021
Cassandra storage times RF:

1189
01:02:01.301 --> 01:02:10.431
3 times replication plus compaction headroom (disk at most 50 to 60 % used), then provision ~2 times the data size.

1190
01:02:10.711 --> 01:02:14.338
Write-priced managed stores (DynamoDB, Keyspaces):

1191
01:02:14.618 --> 01:02:19.973
At 40 K sustained writes per second, per-request pricing can exceed a provisioned cluster.

1192
01:02:20.253 --> 01:02:22.045
Model it (Ch 02).

1193
01:02:22.325 --> 01:02:23.519
Kafka retention:

1194
01:02:23.799 --> 01:02:29.069
3 days times 3 MB per second times RF 3 about 2.3 TB.

1195
01:02:29.349 --> 01:02:31.525
It buys the ability to rebuild stores.

1196
01:02:32.875 --> 01:02:34.219
Operational cost:

1197
01:02:34.499 --> 01:02:39.683
Every new database: on-call runbooks, upgrades, backups, security reviews, skills.

1198
01:02:39.963 --> 01:02:43.099
Often the biggest cost; count it in the decision matrix.

1199
01:02:44.449 --> 01:02:45.387
Part 7.

1200
01:02:45.667 --> 01:02:47.374
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

1201
01:02:48.624 --> 01:02:49.264
1.

1202
01:02:49.544 --> 01:02:53.192
Choosing NoSQL because it scales without access patterns.

1203
01:02:53.472 --> 01:02:54.283
Symptom:

1204
01:02:54.563 --> 01:03:01.539
A document database for relational, transactional data, followed by app-level joins and consistency bugs.

1205
01:03:01.819 --> 01:03:02.544
Fix:

1206
01:03:02.824 --> 01:03:06.792
List the queries and consistency needs first (§2.8).

1207
01:03:07.072 --> 01:03:09.696
The system of record for money stays relational.

1208
01:03:09.976 --> 01:03:10.680
2.

1209
01:03:10.960 --> 01:03:13.947
Modeling Cassandra like a relational database.

1210
01:03:15.297 --> 01:03:26.795
Here is the SQL code. before: normalized tables + ALLOW FILTERING queries = full-cluster scans. after: a table per query, partition key always in WHERE.

1211
01:03:29.645 --> 01:03:30.392
3.

1212
01:03:30.672 --> 01:03:32.101
Unbounded partitions.

1213
01:03:32.381 --> 01:03:33.192
Symptom:

1214
01:03:33.472 --> 01:03:39.232
PRIMARY KEY (riderid, recordedat) produces multi-GB partitions for veteran riders.

1215
01:03:39.512 --> 01:03:40.237
Fix:

1216
01:03:40.517 --> 01:03:41.755
Add a time bucket:

1217
01:03:42.035 --> 01:03:43.933
((riderid, day), recordedat).

1218
01:03:44.213 --> 01:03:44.917
4.

1219
01:03:45.197 --> 01:03:48.269
Using Cassandra (or any LSM store) as a queue.

1220
01:03:48.549 --> 01:03:49.360
Symptom:

1221
01:03:49.640 --> 01:03:54.931
Insert-then-delete per message produces tombstones, and reads slow down until they fail.

1222
01:03:55.211 --> 01:03:55.936
Fix:

1223
01:03:56.216 --> 01:03:58.072
Use Kafka or a queue for queues.

1224
01:03:58.352 --> 01:04:02.683
Use TTL-based expiry with TWCS for time series.

1225
01:04:02.963 --> 01:04:03.731
5.

1226
01:04:04.011 --> 01:04:06.144
Multi-partition BATCH for performance.

1227
01:04:07.494 --> 01:04:20.081
Here is the Java code. before: one coordinator handles 500 partitions, then slower, and one hot node. after: individual async, token-aware, bounded in-flight writes (L3).

1228
01:04:22.931 --> 01:04:23.677
6.

1229
01:04:23.957 --> 01:04:27.285
Redis as a durable database without a rebuild story.

1230
01:04:27.565 --> 01:04:28.376
Symptom:

1231
01:04:28.656 --> 01:04:35.184
A failover loses the last seconds of writes, and it's discovered that it held the only copy of wallet balances.

1232
01:04:35.464 --> 01:04:36.189
Fix:

1233
01:04:36.469 --> 01:04:41.717
Keep only rebuildable or loss-tolerant data in Redis, or data backed by a SoR.

1234
01:04:41.997 --> 01:04:42.744
7.

1235
01:04:43.024 --> 01:04:44.880
KEYS (and friends) in production.

1236
01:04:45.160 --> 01:04:45.971
Symptom:

1237
01:04:46.251 --> 01:04:49.963
The whole Redis shard freezes while it iterates millions of keys.

1238
01:04:50.243 --> 01:04:50.968
Fix:

1239
01:04:51.248 --> 01:04:58.459
SCAN with COUNT, secondary structures (sets or zsets) for lookups, and ACLs that forbid KEYS for app users.

1240
01:04:59.809 --> 01:05:00.491
8.

1241
01:05:00.771 --> 01:05:02.990
Dual writes to several stores from the app.

1242
01:05:03.270 --> 01:05:04.081
Symptom:

1243
01:05:04.361 --> 01:05:07.945
The search index and database disagree forever after a crash.

1244
01:05:08.225 --> 01:05:08.950
Fix:

1245
01:05:09.230 --> 01:05:16.270
One write (DB + outbox, or the log), and independent consumers (§2.10, L4).

1246
01:05:16.550 --> 01:05:17.297
9.

1247
01:05:17.577 --> 01:05:19.475
Locale-dependent machine formats.

1248
01:05:20.825 --> 01:05:31.876
Here is the Java code. before: on a JVM with a comma-decimal default locale, then 12,911600, then unparseable JSON.

1249
01:05:34.726 --> 01:05:35.430
10.

1250
01:05:35.710 --> 01:05:38.142
Embedding unbounded arrays in documents.

1251
01:05:38.422 --> 01:05:42.454
Reviews, events and history belong in their own collection or table.

1252
01:05:43.804 --> 01:05:44.679
Part 8.

1253
01:05:44.959 --> 01:05:46.303
Interview Q and A.

1254
01:05:47.553 --> 01:05:48.385
Beginner.

1255
01:05:48.665 --> 01:05:49.689
Question 1.

1256
01:05:49.969 --> 01:05:52.891
SQL versus NoSQL: how do you choose?

1257
01:05:53.171 --> 01:05:54.537
Thirty-second answer:

1258
01:05:54.817 --> 01:05:58.571
Start from access patterns and consistency needs, not labels.

1259
01:05:58.851 --> 01:06:07.299
Relational databases give transactions, constraints, joins and flexible queries, which suit the system of record for orders and money.

1260
01:06:07.579 --> 01:06:21.766
NoSQL families trade some of that for a specific strength: key-value for sub-ms access, document for self-contained aggregates, wide-column for massive writes by key, graph for traversals, search for relevance.

1261
01:06:23.116 --> 01:06:24.119
Question 2.

1262
01:06:24.399 --> 01:06:27.257
What is Redis good for, and what should you not use it for?

1263
01:06:27.537 --> 01:06:28.903
Thirty-second answer:

1264
01:06:29.183 --> 01:06:42.601
Good for caches, sessions, carts, rate limits, counters, leaderboards, OTPs with TTLs, geo lookups, and lightweight queues or streams: anything needing sub-ms access and rich data structures.

1265
01:06:42.881 --> 01:06:53.057
Not good as the sole store for data you can't lose (acknowledged writes can be lost on failover), or for datasets much larger than memory, or for complex queries.

1266
01:06:54.407 --> 01:06:55.453
Question 3.

1267
01:06:55.733 --> 01:06:59.231
In a document database, when do you embed versus reference?

1268
01:06:59.511 --> 01:07:00.877
Thirty-second answer:

1269
01:07:01.157 --> 01:07:07.258
Embed data that's owned by, bounded with, and read together with the parent (product variants, nutrition).

1270
01:07:07.538 --> 01:07:15.090
Reference data that's shared, unbounded, independently accessed, or frequently changing (brands, reviews, price, stock).

1271
01:07:16.440 --> 01:07:17.507
Question 4.

1272
01:07:17.787 --> 01:07:20.709
Explain the CAP theorem in practical terms.

1273
01:07:20.989 --> 01:07:22.355
Thirty-second answer:

1274
01:07:22.635 --> 01:07:35.264
When a network partition splits a distributed system, each side must choose between returning possibly stale or divergent answers (availability) and refusing requests to stay consistent (consistency).

1275
01:07:35.544 --> 01:07:41.965
Partitions happen, so the choice is really C versus A during partitions, and it can be made per operation.

1276
01:07:43.315 --> 01:07:44.318
Intermediate.

1277
01:07:44.598 --> 01:07:45.707
Question 5.

1278
01:07:45.987 --> 01:07:48.825
Model rider location history in Cassandra.

1279
01:07:49.105 --> 01:07:50.470
Thirty-second answer:

1280
01:07:50.750 --> 01:07:52.179
One table per query.

1281
01:07:52.459 --> 01:07:54.038
For a rider's path for a day:

1282
01:07:54.318 --> 01:08:02.787
PRIMARY KEY ((riderid, day), recordedat) with recordedat DESC clustering, a 7-day TTL, and TWCS compaction.

1283
01:08:03.067 --> 01:08:06.459
For path for an order: a second table keyed by orderid.

1284
01:08:06.739 --> 01:08:10.878
Bucketing by day bounds partitions (~1 MB per rider per day).

1285
01:08:12.228 --> 01:08:13.337
Question 6.

1286
01:08:13.617 --> 01:08:17.863
What is tunable consistency, and what does R + W RF mean?

1287
01:08:18.143 --> 01:08:19.508
Thirty-second answer:

1288
01:08:19.788 --> 01:08:27.617
In Dynamo-style stores you choose per operation how many replicas must acknowledge a write (W) and answer a read (R).

1289
01:08:27.897 --> 01:08:39.417
If R + W RF, every read overlaps at least one replica that has the latest acknowledged write, giving read-your-writes for that key (absent concurrent writes and failures during repair).

1290
01:08:40.767 --> 01:08:41.855
Question 7.

1291
01:08:42.135 --> 01:08:45.101
LSM tree versus B+ tree: trade-offs?

1292
01:08:45.381 --> 01:08:46.746
Thirty-second answer:

1293
01:08:47.026 --> 01:08:50.930
A B+ tree updates in place: cheap reads, moderate writes.

1294
01:08:51.210 --> 01:09:07.295
An LSM tree appends to a memtable, flushes sorted immutable SSTables, and merges them by compaction: very fast writes, but reads may check several files (mitigated by Bloom filters), with space and write amplification from compaction.

1295
01:09:08.645 --> 01:09:09.669
Question 8.

1296
01:09:09.949 --> 01:09:13.171
How would you find the nearest available riders to a dark store?

1297
01:09:13.451 --> 01:09:14.816
Thirty-second answer:

1298
01:09:15.096 --> 01:09:19.128
Keep the latest positions in a geo index (Redis GEO) per city.

1299
01:09:19.408 --> 01:09:24.336
GEOSEARCH by radius ascending with a small count, then filter by availability.

1300
01:09:24.616 --> 01:09:32.147
Prune riders not seen for 60 s, and guard updates with sequence numbers so out-of-order pings don't regress positions.

1301
01:09:33.497 --> 01:09:34.563
Question 9.

1302
01:09:34.843 --> 01:09:39.238
When is PostgreSQL JSONB enough, and when would you pick MongoDB?

1303
01:09:39.518 --> 01:09:40.883
Thirty-second answer:

1304
01:09:41.163 --> 01:09:49.291
JSONB is enough when document data sits alongside relational data, needs transactions with it, and fits one primary's scale.

1305
01:09:49.571 --> 01:10:03.865
Choose MongoDB (or similar) when the workload is primarily document-shaped at a scale needing native sharding, when teams benefit from its tooling and developer model, or when flexible schemas across many teams dominate.

1306
01:10:05.215 --> 01:10:05.983
Senior.

1307
01:10:06.263 --> 01:10:07.308
Question 10.

1308
01:10:07.588 --> 01:10:09.935
How do you detect and fix a hot partition?

1309
01:10:10.215 --> 01:10:11.580
Thirty-second answer:

1310
01:10:11.860 --> 01:10:19.071
Detect it with uneven node metrics, nodetool toppartitions, per-partition latency, or cloud hot key metrics.

1311
01:10:19.351 --> 01:10:33.367
Fix it by changing the partition key to spread load (add a bucket such as time or a hash suffix), splitting hot keys into N sub-keys and merging on read, caching hot reads, or rate-limiting abusive producers.

1312
01:10:34.717 --> 01:10:35.847
Question 11.

1313
01:10:36.127 --> 01:10:38.922
What are tombstones, and why do they cause outages?

1314
01:10:39.202 --> 01:10:40.567
Thirty-second answer:

1315
01:10:40.847 --> 01:10:50.853
Deletes (and expired TTLs) in LSM stores write tombstone markers that shadow older data until compaction removes them after gcgraceseconds.

1316
01:10:51.133 --> 01:10:58.023
Reads over ranges full of tombstones must scan them all, which slows or fails queries (tombstone thresholds).

1317
01:10:58.303 --> 01:11:01.461
Queue-like or delete-heavy patterns are the usual cause.

1318
01:11:02.811 --> 01:11:03.899
Question 12.

1319
01:11:04.179 --> 01:11:07.784
How do you do atomic multi-key operations in Redis Cluster?

1320
01:11:08.064 --> 01:11:09.429
Thirty-second answer:

1321
01:11:09.709 --> 01:11:14.253
Multi-key commands and Lua scripts require all keys in one hash slot.

1322
01:11:14.533 --> 01:11:21.424
Use hash tags (BLR:...) to co-locate related keys, then use MULTI or EXEC or Lua for atomicity.

1323
01:11:21.704 --> 01:11:23.581
Otherwise you get CROSSSLOT errors.

1324
01:11:24.931 --> 01:11:26.211
Question 13.

1325
01:11:26.491 --> 01:11:30.971
How do you keep multiple stores (DB, cache, search, Cassandra) consistent?

1326
01:11:31.251 --> 01:11:32.617
Thirty-second answer:

1327
01:11:32.897 --> 01:11:34.369
Don't dual-write from the app.

1328
01:11:34.649 --> 01:11:47.193
Write once to the system of record (DB + transactional outbox, or an event log), then feed each derived store from that log with its own consumer, using idempotent writes and replay for recovery.

1329
01:11:47.473 --> 01:11:49.947
Accept and measure eventual consistency.

1330
01:11:51.297 --> 01:11:52.492
Question 14.

1331
01:11:52.772 --> 01:11:57.935
Migrate rider locations from PostgreSQL to Cassandra with zero downtime.

1332
01:11:58.215 --> 01:11:59.580
Thirty-second answer:

1333
01:11:59.860 --> 01:12:08.393
Introduce the log first: ingest publishes to Kafka while still writing PostgreSQL (or CDC from PostgreSQL).

1334
01:12:08.673 --> 01:12:14.113
Stand up Cassandra consumers from the log and backfill recent history from PostgreSQL.

1335
01:12:14.393 --> 01:12:16.975
Verify with shadow reads comparing results.

1336
01:12:17.255 --> 01:12:24.039
Switch reads behind a flag, stop the PostgreSQL writes, then drop the table (after a retention period).

1337
01:12:25.389 --> 01:12:26.285
Architect.

1338
01:12:26.565 --> 01:12:27.759
Question 15.

1339
01:12:28.039 --> 01:12:30.301
A team wants a new database for a feature.

1340
01:12:30.581 --> 01:12:32.010
How do you run the decision?

1341
01:12:32.290 --> 01:12:33.655
Thirty-second answer:

1342
01:12:33.935 --> 01:12:51.365
Require a short design doc: access patterns with volumes and SLOs, consistency needs, growth, the system of record, rebuild story, and a weighted decision matrix (fit, consistency, scale, operability, cost, ecosystem) including use an existing store.

1343
01:12:51.645 --> 01:12:58.706
Prefer existing or managed stores unless the gap is large, and record the deciding factor in an ADR.

1344
01:13:00.056 --> 01:13:01.251
Question 16.

1345
01:13:01.531 --> 01:13:05.435
Managed versus self-hosted NoSQL: how do you decide?

1346
01:13:05.715 --> 01:13:07.080
Thirty-second answer:

1347
01:13:07.360 --> 01:13:15.488
Managed services remove patching, backups, failover and scaling toil at higher unit cost and with some feature or version limits.

1348
01:13:15.768 --> 01:13:27.331
Self-hosting gives control and possibly lower cost at large scale, but requires deep expertise (repairs, compaction tuning, upgrades) and 24 times 7 on-call.

1349
01:13:27.611 --> 01:13:34.096
For most teams, managed wins until scale economics and in-house expertise justify otherwise.

1350
01:13:35.446 --> 01:13:36.790
Question 17.

1351
01:13:37.070 --> 01:13:41.635
How do you set consistency expectations per data class across the platform?

1352
01:13:41.915 --> 01:13:43.281
Thirty-second answer:

1353
01:13:43.561 --> 01:13:44.841
Classify the data.

1354
01:13:45.121 --> 01:13:52.331
Money and inventory: strong consistency in the SoR (PostgreSQL), with idempotent, auditable writes.

1355
01:13:52.611 --> 01:13:57.369
User-visible state after own writes: read-your-writes (primary reads or tokens).

1356
01:13:57.649 --> 01:14:01.595
Browsing and search: bounded staleness (seconds, minutes), measured.

1357
01:14:01.875 --> 01:14:03.326
Analytics: hours.

1358
01:14:03.606 --> 01:14:06.465
Locations: seconds-stale, loss-tolerant.

1359
01:14:06.745 --> 01:14:09.198
Document it and give each class SLOs.

1360
01:14:10.548 --> 01:14:11.721
Question 18.

1361
01:14:12.001 --> 01:14:16.375
What is DynamoDB single-table design, and when is it a good idea?

1362
01:14:16.655 --> 01:14:18.020
Thirty-second answer:

1363
01:14:18.300 --> 01:14:35.900
Storing multiple entity types in one table with overloaded partition and sort keys (PK=CUSTOMER123, SK=ORDER2026 to 09 to 26ord9) and GSIs, so that each access pattern is one query.

1364
01:14:36.180 --> 01:14:42.409
It's good when access patterns are known and stable and very high scale with predictable latency matters.

1365
01:14:42.689 --> 01:14:47.532
It's painful when access patterns change often or ad hoc queries are needed.

1366
01:14:48.882 --> 01:14:50.119
Question 19.

1367
01:14:50.399 --> 01:14:52.405
Your Redis cluster lost all data.

1368
01:14:52.685 --> 01:14:56.055
Walk through recovery and what the design should have guaranteed.

1369
01:14:56.335 --> 01:14:57.701
Thirty-second answer:

1370
01:14:57.981 --> 01:15:00.967
Immediately: the platform should degrade, not fail.

1371
01:15:01.247 --> 01:15:08.757
Carts fall back to the SoR snapshot, rate limits fail open with conservative defaults, and sessions force re-login.

1372
01:15:09.037 --> 01:15:19.362
Rebuild derived state from the SoR or the log: replay loc-latest from Kafka offsets a few minutes back, and warm caches gradually to avoid stampedes.

1373
01:15:19.642 --> 01:15:22.927
After recovery, audit which data had no rebuild path.

1374
01:15:24.277 --> 01:15:25.387
Question 20.

1375
01:15:25.667 --> 01:15:27.544
How many databases is too many?

1376
01:15:27.824 --> 01:15:29.189
Thirty-second answer:

1377
01:15:29.469 --> 01:15:39.667
When the operational load (expertise, on-call, upgrades, security, backups) exceeds the team's capacity, or when new stores are added for marginal gains.

1378
01:15:39.947 --> 01:15:53.835
A healthy platform has a small paved road: a relational SoR, a cache or key-value store, a search engine, a log (Kafka), and perhaps one analytical or wide-column store, each with owners and runbooks.

1379
01:15:55.185 --> 01:15:56.081
Part 9.

1380
01:15:56.361 --> 01:15:57.769
Summary and Practice.

1381
01:15:59.019 --> 01:16:00.107
Key takeaways.

1382
01:16:00.387 --> 01:16:04.909
Choose stores by access pattern, scale and consistency, not by label.

1383
01:16:05.189 --> 01:16:07.451
Keep a single system of record per fact.

1384
01:16:07.731 --> 01:16:13.149
Key-value (Redis) gives sub-ms structures with TTLs, but treat it as rebuildable.

1385
01:16:13.429 --> 01:16:15.691
Hash-tag keys for cluster atomicity.

1386
01:16:15.971 --> 01:16:20.835
Documents: embed what's owned, bounded and read together, and reference the rest.

1387
01:16:21.115 --> 01:16:22.629
JSONB is often enough.

1388
01:16:22.909 --> 01:16:33.491
Wide-column: one table per query, the partition key in every query, bounded partitions (time buckets), TTL + TWCS for time series.

1389
01:16:34.841 --> 01:16:43.054
LSM trees make writes cheap with memtables, SSTables and compaction, at the cost of read, space and write amplification.

1390
01:16:43.334 --> 01:16:46.811
Bloom filters and compaction strategy are the tuning levers.

1391
01:16:47.091 --> 01:16:53.278
CAP is about partitions, and PACELC adds latency versus consistency the rest of the time.

1392
01:16:53.558 --> 01:16:57.889
Consistency is tunable per operation (R + W RF).

1393
01:16:58.169 --> 01:16:59.278
No dual writes.

1394
01:16:59.558 --> 01:17:05.105
One write to the log or SoR, and independent, idempotent consumers per derived store.

1395
01:17:05.385 --> 01:17:10.078
Out-of-order updates need sequence or version guards, especially during replays.

1396
01:17:11.428 --> 01:17:13.988
The decision matrix must weight operability.

1397
01:17:14.268 --> 01:17:17.063
Near-ties call for naming the deciding factor.

1398
01:17:17.343 --> 01:17:21.567
Every store needs a failure and rebuild story before it goes to production.

1399
01:17:22.917 --> 01:17:24.239
